I didn't know dr. King but what he did  and what he did for me and what he did  for us changed my life I came into the  army at a time when segregation was  still in the land although the army had  integrated but we had this dichotomy  between being on an army post where I  was open and being in Columbus Georgia  where I was prejudiced where people  threw me out of a hamburger joint after  I came back from my first tour in  Vietnam and had been injured and all I  wanted was a hammer and they wouldn't  serve me but it was dr. King and his  efforts beginning from the 1955 period  the Montgomery bus boycott all the way  through his death in 1968 it was his  efforts that not only freed blacks it  freely freed Americans and freed whites  it took a burden off the white people of  the United States and took a burden off  the black people so it was something he  did for all of America and that's what  made his contribution is so significant  and even though I couldn't participate  in that because I was an active duty  officer going to Vietnam twice during  that period and also serving elsewhere I  followed it every time I went to Vietnam  I left my wife and infant children back  in Birmingham Alabama and in 1963  Birmingham Alabama was not a very nice  place at riots Bull Connor and his dogs  and my father-in-law spent his time  guarding my family while I was in  Vietnam serving the nation and all that  changed now dr. King's legacy is a real  one we've got a lot more to do without a  long way to go but I'm sitting areas and  son of an immigrant family a black kid  public school education was able to rise  to the top and the way that was made for  me was built a large extent by what dr.  Cain did and by his great sacrifice when  he was murdered on April 4th 1968 I was  at Fort Leavenworth Kansas in training  getting ready to go back to Vietnam I'll  never forget the shock that went through  our community and I'll never forget the  image of buses being put around the  White House bumper to bumper  check the White House from the outraged  citizens in Washington DC and so he will  never be forgotten the only thing I was  able to do later in life was to be on a  Council that helped build a new monument  to dr. King but he had an indelible  impression upon America and he should  always be treasured for that and we  should all pause and reflect on the  contributions he's made on this 50th  anniversary of his passing it's hard to  tell what the next 50 years will be like  because I couldn't tell you 50 years ago  what today would be like we've done  thinkable for me to become chairman of  the Joint Chiefs of Staff or Secretary  of State but the trend I see is that  America is becoming a more mosaic nation  by that I mean you see in two marriages  you see tolerance of marriages that you  would not have seen to be tolerance as  years ago so I think America by the Year  2043 will find that the minorities of  Americans are now the majority of  Americans and we're the only nation in  the world who could handle this kind of  demographic change and I'm confident as  I watch television and kind of don't pay  attention to the wild stuff that said  the watch television more and more I see  acceptance of this kind of change in a  demographics and I hope that 50 years  from now we will all be again one people  one nation one nation under God as our  founding fathers told us we should be  and that is my hope and I think that's  dr. King's hope if he was so alive he  would be beating up everybody to do the  right thing he would not have given up  remember he died just after going to the  service of garbage men in Memphis the  whole nation was carrying the burden of  discrimination and Jim Crow ISM it was  holding blacks down but it's also  holding whites down they knew better  they knew this was wrong they knew that  the Emancipation Proclamation and  Abraham Lincoln what he did the Civil  War they knew that was the way to go but  for many years after that throughout the  rest of that century blacks were again  subjected there was slaves once again  even if there was not legal slavery  and so the story I always like to tell  is the hamburger joint I got thrown out  of after I came back from Vietnam was  looking for a place for my family to  live I just went in for hamburger I knew  I was in a segregated City and she  wouldn't serve me but I went back to  that same hamburger joint on July 4th  1964 right after the civil rights  accommodation Act had been signed and I  asked my hamburger and they said yes sir  and they handed me my hamburger she  didn't want to discriminate against me  and we removed that burden off of her I  could go into a store now and try and  close all the signs that used to exist  all over the south anywhere below  Washington DC would have three signs  over the three restrooms in a gas  station black and then white men white  women blacks going all going you  couldn't find any of those in the  country now and so they were suffering  too there's still some of them who think  well we should go a little bit backwards  no we're going to keep going forward but  it's a challenge and it's a struggle and  all of us have to fight this struggle to  make sure that all of our fellow  citizens not just blacks but Indians  Hispanics and anybody else you can think  of is treated equally and gets just  results from their work their education  and their belief in this great country  of ours I think we've come a long way  but we've got a long way to go  we see everyday problems that exists in  our country but at the same time I see  great things that have happened in our  country I see a black mayor in Plano  Texas something you never would have  thought would happen I've seen  immigrants rising to top positions I see  people of color who are very very  wealthy in the stock market  I see people of color and different  backgrounds different different sources  of their coming to the United States  doing very very well I'm moving up so  you can't say that it's all the failure  what it has been is great success but  success doesn't end you have to keep  going after it you have to keep finding  where the problems are where the  discrimination is and doing something  about it and when I think dr. King will  be focusing on right now is the people  who are the poorest in our land  who do not have the education they need  who are not living in the kind of family  units that they really need and who need  jobs ultimately the solution to all  problems associated with our nation I  think is to get everybody an education  and a job and put them in a family unit  that can bring the children up raise the  children and teach children how to mind  their manners and mind their adults and  that's what it's all about
 

